data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering 31 bytes per transaction (PUT + COMMIT) implies at most ~540,000 writes in total, the persistent AVL tree's logarithmic growth means around 18 nodes per update. Java's garbage collection should readily handle the intermediate node cleanup during commits, as unreferenced nodes become eligible immediately, potentially avoiding significant memory overhead. However, it's still worth examining options to minimize object allocation.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"If allocations take ~50ms, optimization is crucial, especially regarding refCount/version. Each node could store a version (commit sequence) incremented only during BEGIN operations, indicating the transaction's snapshot. If no transaction is currently open, or all open transactions have a snapshot version less than the current epoch, nodes can be mutated in place after a commit without needing to copy the data, minimizing allocation overhead.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The critical insight is that transaction commits happen *after* the transaction is closed, and only *open* transactions require snapshots.  Node versioning using the epoch of the current root allows in-place updates after a transaction commits if no open transactions reference that version. The persistent AVL tree structure, simplified with primitive data types, seems viable for efficient state management with minimal memory overhead.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

