data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict detection involves $O((|readSet| + |readRanges|) \\log N)$ complexity at transaction commit. This is due to checking the read set and ranges against the `LastWriteTree`. The crucial point is that committing transactions do NOT scan the open transaction. During the open transaction's read/range operations, each READ/RANGE has $O(\\log M)$ complexity, considering the transaction's snapshot.\n\nThe total number of READ and RANGE records across all transactions within the 16 MiB log is approximately 1,400,000.  At commit, each read and range in `LastWriteTree` is O(log N). The focus is now to confirm that the `maxCommitSeqInRange` query (or the optimized `hasCommitAfter` with early pruning) within the augmented BST provides O(log N) lookup.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The range query must be optimized to ensure O(log N) complexity. Preliminary analysis suggests that, without subtree bounds, a naive implementation would lead to unnecessary traversal. The introduction of bounded ranges (loUnbounded, hiUnbounded) allows for crucial pruning during recursion, optimizing the range max query. This approach maintains logarithmic complexity. The algorithm's effectiveness in pruning subtrees guarantees O(log N) lookup.\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The range query's complexity hinges on pruning, ensuring O(log N). Bounded ranges enable recursion pruning, optimizing range max queries. The proposed comparison-based approach facilitates logarithmic complexity by efficiently restricting traversal within the tree. Specifically, the checks determine if a key `K` within a range [lo, hi] has a sequence `seq` > `minSeq`, with efficient pruning that avoids scanning unnecessary subtrees.\n\nThe critical observation is that each comparison leads to either a direct match (immediate return if a conflict exists) or a recursive call restricted by the range bounds. Recursively calling the left child only happens if the range's lower bound is less than the current node's key. Recursively calling the right child only happens if the range's upper bound is greater than the current node's key. This pruning approach ensures logarithmic time for `hasCommitAfter` verification.\n\nThe worst case is when the range encompasses a significant portion of the tree; the method will still ensure pruning, leading to O(log N) operations. The method efficiently checks for a conflict within a range using these comparisons and pruning.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The range query’s efficiency relies on the recursive calls and how `hiUnbounded` and `loUnbounded` are managed. At the split node, the left call sets `hiUnbounded = true` and the right call sets `loUnbounded = true`. This leads to a single root-to-leaf traversal on the left and right, with a constant number of checks at each step, ensuring a worst-case $O(\\log N)$ performance. Specifically, `hasCommitAfter` visits at most $4 \\times height$ nodes due to pruning. This $O(\\log N)$ efficiency depends on the proper management of the range bounds during the recursive calls. Also, if there is no conflict, the algorithm returns `false` at the root node in $O(1)$.\n\nThe conflict detection is analyzed in detail. The conditions of the conflict are precisely examined per R22.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict detection criteria explicitly states that only *committed* transactions trigger conflicts. An aborted transaction has no impact. Now focusing on cases where a conflict is triggered. If transaction `U` begins before `T` begins, but `U` commits after `T` begins, then this still meets the condition where `U.commitSeq > T.beginCommitSeq` in the proposed system.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

