data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The key insight is that only committed transactions contribute to conflict detection; aborted transactions are irrelevant, which aligns with R22. If a transaction `U` writes key `K` and then rolls back that write, R22 and Section 4 confirm that the `writeList` at commit includes only non-rolled-back writes, ensuring correct conflict detection. This means during COMMIT, only non-rolled-back writes are considered, which ensures correct conflict detection. Even a DELETE of a non-existent key, although a no-op, is a write and therefore must be in the `writeList`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The critical insight is the `writeList`'s role in conflict detection.  Even a DELETE operation, even of a non-existent key, counts as a \"write\" and updates the `LastWriteTree`. This aligns with the definition of conflicts. For the conflict: consider transaction `U` writing `K=\"1\"` after transaction `T` begins, followed by `U2` deleting `K` before `T` commits. If `T` then reads `K`, a conflict MUST exist since both `U1` and `U2` wrote `K`, and R22 mandates conflict. The `LastWriteTree` correctly reflects this conflict because `lastCommitSeq(K)` will be `U2.commitSeq`, which is greater than `T.beginCommitSeq`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The read set only includes keys from READ operations, even if the key exists in the transaction's write list. A read key is always added to the transaction's read set, ensuring conflict detection is correct. The read ranges are only included when the lower bound is less than or equal to the higher bound.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Now reviewing the provided API and rules for compliance. The API defines a `recover` method, taking a byte array `log` as input, and returning a `Result` record. The `Result` record encapsulates the recovered state, committed transaction IDs, aborted transaction IDs, incomplete transaction IDs, and information about the valid log length and whether a torn tail exists. The `Code` enum defines various error conditions that can arise during recovery. The code is carefully designed to be immutable and uses `final` classes, `record`, and `enum` to ensure the integrity of the data structures and prevent unintended modifications, also preventing exceptions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `Code` enum's order and names are verified against the provided documentation, confirming a complete and exact match of 17 constants. The data structures returned in the `Result` record are being evaluated for mutability considerations. The goal is to determine whether these collections, specifically `state`, `committed`, `aborted`, and `incomplete`, should be mutable (using standard `HashMap`/`ArrayList`) or unmodifiable (using `Collections.emptyMap()`/`List.of()`) to align with design principles and testing requirements, particularly the comparison of the `state` as a set.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

