data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial checks confirm validity and prevent out-of-bounds access within the recovery process. Record type validation against defined constants is performed, followed by checks on record length ensuring an upper bound. A crucial memory safety optimization prevents potential integer overflows during length calculations, safeguarding against arbitrary large log input. The Fletcher-16 checksum verification is the next step to confirm data integrity.\n\nFletcher-16 checksum optimization is in progress. The analysis continues, precisely examining the checksum calculation and its accurate implementation. The algorithm is analyzed to determine if the checksum is calculated and validated correctly. The goal is a highly precise and optimized implementation. The focus now is on understanding and verifying the integrity of the Fletcher-16 checksum calculations.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The Fletcher-16 checksum optimization is now clear: modular reduction is applied *after* each byte, keeping intermediate sums within a small range. This avoids potential integer overflows. The detailed examination confirms this method is mathematically sound, using Java's modulo operator to guarantee correctness. It has been validated that `(a + b) % 255 == ((a % 255) + b) % 255` is a mathematically accurate identity for all non-negative integers. This preserves modular arithmetic properties. The use of modulo 255 always yields results in the range [0, 254], preventing errors.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

