data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Value byte validation now implemented for PUT and READ records, enforcing `0x20 <= b <= 0x7E` constraints. Implemented `BAD_VALUE` exception. SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK name byte validation added, enforcing `0x21 <= name <= 0x7E` and raising `BAD_NAME` exception when invalid. Validated the correct exception order: R13 -> R14 -> R15 -> R16 -> R16a. The transaction checks and actions for R17-R23e are the current focus. Considering how to track seen BEGIN transaction IDs to handle DUPLICATE_TXN (R17).\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"An efficient `IntHashSet` using an `int[]` for storing transaction IDs is the optimal choice, since transaction IDs are non-zero.  An open-addressing hash table with zero as the empty slot sentinel avoids `Long` boxing, significantly reducing memory overhead.  The initial capacity is determined directly from `validLength` (total log bytes) to prevent resizing.  Given the size of each `BEGIN` record, the maximum expected number of `BEGIN` records is calculated from `validLength` to derive the most optimal power-of-2 size, preventing rehash and lowering the collision possibility.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `IntHashSet`'s size is tailored precisely to the log size, minimizing memory usage with no object allocations via optimized hashing, providing deterministic behavior for txn ID tracking.  Now, focusing on open transactions, a fixed-size array (`openTxns`) of size four, coupled with a count (`openCount`), efficiently manages open transactions, guaranteeing a maximum of four concurrent transactions and ensuring performance.  Maintaining `openTxns` sorted by transaction ID enables ordered checkpoints per R19c.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

