data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `openTxns` array, bounded at four entries, will be maintained as a sorted list by transaction ID to optimize checkpoint validation (R19c) and incomplete transaction reporting (R26). The `BEGIN`, `COMMIT`, and `ABORT` operations will maintain the sorted nature with minimal shifting due to the limited size. Checkpoint validation will efficiently verify the listed transaction IDs. The `incomplete` list can be constructed directly from the sorted `openTxns` array at the end of phase 2. Considering reusing a pool of four `Txn` objects to minimize memory allocation as only a maximum of four transactions can be open at any time.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A fixed-size array (`openTxns`) and a separate counter (`openCount`) manage open transactions, ensuring a maximum of four concurrent transactions with sorted transaction IDs. This optimizes checkpoint validation (R19c) and reduces incomplete transaction reporting (R26) with minimal shifting during BEGIN, COMMIT, and ABORT operations.  A pool of four `Txn` objects minimizes memory allocation by reusing objects.\n\nMemory is being optimized for `Txn` objects. By replacing large lists like `readKeys` with a new, smaller `ArrayList` if `readKeys.size() > 1024` during reset, peak heap usage can be limited. The implementation focuses on managing `Txn` state and operations, including txn id, snapshot root, beginCommitSeq, and the write list (max 8 writes).\n\nThe `Txn`'s internal structure is being examined:  `id`, `snapshotRoot`, `beginCommitSeq`, and the `writeList` (containing `writeKeys`, `writeValues`, and `writeCount`). The use of `null` for `DELETE` operations is confirmed, and a non-null string, including empty strings, indicates a `PUT` operation.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `writeValues` array's `null` values are confirmed as representing `DELETE` operations, while non-null values indicate `PUT` operations, including empty strings for valid put operations. The 94 possible savepoint names are confirmed. Now, reviewing R23a and R23b related to SAVEPOINT and how a savepoint's position is updated by the savepoint record.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R23a (SAVEPOINT) and R23b (ROLLBACK) logic are now examined closely. Savepoint management is critical, especially due to the limited number of possible names. R23a confirms savepoints are added based on offset and saved names. R23b (ROLLBACK) is examined. The algorithm preserves the initial *n* writes, discards the rest from the write list, and removes subsequent savepoints in order of offset, keeping the named savepoint. The process maintains transaction state and only changes the write list and saves for the transaction with the matching savepoint name.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A compact array of at most 94 active savepoints, ordered by offset, minimizes memory overhead for SAVEPOINT/ROLLBACK operations. Instead of an array, a direct-indexed table (`spIndexByName[94]`) tracks the index of each savepoint name or `-1` if absent. This approach avoids gaps in a stack-like structure, ensuring efficient savepoint management. SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK operations will update savepoint offsets and names, maintaining a compact, ordered representation. The `spOffset[94]` array, initialized to -1, handles absent savepoint entries.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

