data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A fixed-size array (`spOffset`) of size 94, indexed by savepoint name, directly tracks savepoint offsets, improving access time. The `SAVEPOINT` operation performs constant-time updates to this array and the `activeNames` list, which is an `int[]` for storing indexes (savepoint name - 0x21). `ROLLBACK` efficiently finds the target offset and discards writes/savepoints beyond it. The savepoint management ensures efficient lookups and updates within the transaction, with savepoints sorted by name. The operations' time complexity is $O(1)$ for LOOKUP. `ROLLBACK` removes savepoints after the targeted one. This ensures constant lookup cost for savepoint handling.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`ROLLBACK` and reset performance is now analyzed.  `SAVEPOINT` remains $O(1)$.  `ROLLBACK` involves at most 94 integer comparisons ($O(1)$) and `Txn` reset involves at most 94 integer writes ($O(1)$) due to savepoint management structure. The critical R23b edge case analysis reveals that the target savepoint persists, maintaining its index and offset.  Savepoint offsets are strictly unique due to record offsets. The analysis confirms R23a consistency and rollback behavior.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-01da08e8-1ab6-4371-ad44-742764e18821","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795549634,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A `ROLLBACK` operation, followed by a new `SAVEPOINT` with the same name, requires careful handling of savepoint offsets and write lists. Specifically, considering how the `writeCount` of the transaction could be less than the saved index's position after a rollback and before a new savepoint. Examining edge cases and tracing changes in savepoint index (`idx`) and write count across rollback and savepoint operations is crucial. Initializing a new `SAVEPOINT` is now confirmed.\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

